window.docContents[115].resolve({"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Overloading-and-Type-Classes--Summary--Deriving-Instances":{"contents":"Many type classes have very standard implementations.\nFor instance, the Boolean equality class  is usually implemented by first checking whether both arguments are built with the same constructor, and then checking whether all their arguments are equal.\nInstances for these classes can be created automatically.\n\nWhen defining an inductive type or a structure, a deriving clause at the end of the declaration will cause instances to be created automatically.\nAdditionally, the deriving instance﻿ ... ﻿for﻿ ... command can be used outside of the definition of a datatype to cause an instance to be generated.\nBecause each class for which instances can be derived requires special handling, not all classes are derivable.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Overloading and Type Classes\u0009Summary","header":"3.8.4. Deriving Instances","id":"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Overloading-and-Type-Classes--Summary--Deriving-Instances"}});